WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.639
A human password has a different

2
00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:05.313
security profile from a randomly generated
key.

3
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:08.343
Password choices have limited, uneven
entropy, so storing

4
00:00:08.343 --> 00:00:11.373
an ordinary fast digest leaves
verification guesses inexpensive.

5
00:00:11.373 --> 00:00:14.226
Password storage should use a dedicated
construction such

6
00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:16.810
as Argon2id or scrypt, with a fresh

7
00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.840
salt for each password and deliberately
expensive computation.

8
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.406
Select supported parameters for the
deployment and benchmark them under

9
00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:26.792
realistic load rather than copying an
unexplained cost setting.

10
00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:29.288
Salts are normally stored with the
password

11
00:00:29.288 --> 00:00:31.962
hash and its algorithm and cost metadata.

12
00:00:31.962 --> 00:00:36.418
They are not secret encryption keys and do
not encrypt the password.

13
00:00:36.418 --> 00:00:38.914
Their purpose includes making otherwise
identical passwords

14
00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:41.053
have distinct stored results and
preventing

15
00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:43.726
useful sharing of precomputed work across
accounts.

16
00:00:43.726 --> 00:00:45.865
Let a maintained password hashing
interface

17
00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:48.183
generate and encode the required salt.

18
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:50.322
Preserve versioned parameters so
successful verification

19
00:00:50.322 --> 00:00:52.996
can trigger an upgrade when policy
changes.

20
00:00:52.996 --> 00:00:57.095
Password based key derivation is related
but serves a different purpose.

21
00:00:57.095 --> 00:01:01.463
If the application truly needs a key
derived from a human password,

22
00:01:01.463 --> 00:01:06.454
use a password KDF with an appropriate
salt, work factors, and output length.

23
00:01:06.454 --> 00:01:11.267
This does not turn a weak password into a
high entropy random secret.

24
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:14.119
Password changes and recovery must be
designed around

25
00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:17.060
how the resulting key protects data,
because changing

26
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.090
the password can otherwise make earlier
ciphertext inaccessible.

27
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.833
Deriving subkeys from strong secret key
material is another case.

28
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:28.201
A construction such as HKDF can produce
context separated keys, but it

29
00:01:28.201 --> 00:01:32.657
is not an expensive password hashing
function and does not replace one.

30
00:01:32.657 --> 00:01:35.955
Domain separation gives different roles
explicit context, such as

31
00:01:35.955 --> 00:01:39.341
encryption versus message authentication
or one protocol versus another.

32
00:01:39.341 --> 00:01:41.481
Use independently generated keys or a

33
00:01:41.481 --> 00:01:44.154
reviewed derivation scheme with distinct
purpose labels.

34
00:01:44.154 --> 00:01:45.759
Separate environments as well.

35
00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:48.254
Reusing one key everywhere makes unrelated
cryptographic

36
00:01:48.254 --> 00:01:51.106
roles share failure and lifecycle
assumptions that the

37
00:01:51.106 --> 00:01:53.780
application may never have intended to
connect.

